#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d14dbef-9a16-537e-a182-87fd6853e543" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "fc06df19-d6de-5730-a239-fe02cc42cd59" :question/math-academy-id "e-643" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\n- $a_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}$ for $n \\geq 1$\n\n- $a_n  = \\dfrac{n^4}{2n^3+1}$ for $n \\geq 1$\n\n- $a_n = \\dfrac {3 n ^ 5 + 7} {5 n ^ 4 - 3 n ^ 3 }$ for $n\\geq 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches at infinity.\n\n- In sequence I, the limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\dfrac {1}{n^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence II, the dominant term in the denominator is $2n^3,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{2{n}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(2+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{2+0} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is $5n^4,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n^4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{n}^{5}+7}{5{n}^{4}-3{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{5{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3n+\\frac{7}{{n}^{4}})}{(5-\\frac{3}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3n+0}{5-0} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequences II and III diverge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bec57d9-3abf-58cb-87cb-1449224e6c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Convergent Rational Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "0615e448-c18c-5f47-aa1b-18f0045cbbea" :question/math-academy-id "e-644" :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac {3 n ^ 5 - n} {5 n ^ 6  - n}$ for $n\\geq 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether  the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\dfrac {3 n ^ 5 - n} {5 n ^ 6  - n}\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $5n^6,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n^6$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{n}^{5}-n}{5{n}^{6}-n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{n}^{5}}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{n}{{n}^{6}})}{(\\frac{5{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{n}{{n}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{n}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{5-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096550 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46630d98-f27d-5426-aaad-0c390078e9b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of a Factored Rational Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "5de361e2-c574-556d-80ef-e7d75ffa93fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-645" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac {n(3n-7)} {(3n-1)(3n+1)}$ for $n\\geq 1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac {n(3n-7)} {(3n-1)(3n+1)}=\\dfrac {3 n ^ 2 - 7 n} {9 n ^ 2-1}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {3 n ^ 2 - 7 n} {9 n ^ 2-1}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $n^2,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7n}{9{n}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{7n}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3-\\frac{7}{n})}{(9-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{9-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $\\dfrac 1 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bec57d9-3abf-58cb-87cb-1449224e6c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Convergent Rational Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "653f6467-cb55-5bf5-b8da-34a8359febd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257670} {:answer/id #uuid "77d0807b-c761-579b-a118-e06f29841874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257842} {:answer/id #uuid "872ddb92-7c75-5904-8290-de2c702ce506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257967} {:answer/id #uuid "b73ab00d-cd82-5fc1-97be-3ee660fecad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258379} {:answer/id #uuid "f4390655-5558-5a15-a8f7-cb114f56835d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73ab00d-cd82-5fc1-97be-3ee660fecad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258379} :answer-field/id #uuid "737d29c7-7ede-5b85-bd2b-c8b1c43bd2bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58fed700-65f7-5f8a-af51-e2d67f4fc4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123307" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}}{5-2{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}}{5-2{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-2{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}}{5-2{n}^{4}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{5}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{\\frac{5}{{n}^{4}}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bec57d9-3abf-58cb-87cb-1449224e6c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Convergent Rational Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a54534-4767-584b-952d-179fe726d8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257519} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb20bbd-10f5-54ee-a55f-29474bcb1280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257909} {:answer/id #uuid "a243f9fd-d950-5b6f-87be-03e71163316e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258202} {:answer/id #uuid "c84d2d6f-3608-5c23-8857-fcd382967d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258529} {:answer/id #uuid "f62f132c-4e63-56d3-a77f-a6d972cb0b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a54534-4767-584b-952d-179fe726d8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257519} :answer-field/id #uuid "054b45ed-9ddd-5fb1-b64c-65e2f755ca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc79d507-20ac-5720-b3d4-b351fd9c3cbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-123309" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2n+1}{7n-5{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\frac{2n+1}{7n-5{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-5{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2n+1}{7n-5{n}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{7n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{\\frac{7}{n}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d14dbef-9a16-537e-a182-87fd6853e543" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c66940e-cbcf-514e-b9e2-e24bae4a7292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379330} {:answer/id #uuid "54826272-7f98-55af-96d9-094453fc0efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379331} {:answer/id #uuid "28effab1-bccc-5cc1-8889-acb2d7cdf1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379332} {:answer/id #uuid "471d2065-290f-5a12-be12-38eb90e6c4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379333} {:answer/id #uuid "6af58cb7-f888-59dc-a085-19e95938c871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6af58cb7-f888-59dc-a085-19e95938c871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379334} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff130ac6-42d2-501c-9704-3a06364e2770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0f1cd4a-9b86-572b-aad9-1589be5a377d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18689" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8{n}^{6}+4{n}^{5}}{8{n}^{4}+10}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The dominant term in the denominator is $8{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{6}+4{n}^{5}}{8{n}^{4}+10} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{n}^{6}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{4{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{8{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{10}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{2}+4n}{(8+\\frac{10}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{2}+4n}{8+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }({n}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}n) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bec57d9-3abf-58cb-87cb-1449224e6c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Convergent Rational Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a91a69b5-6f3d-57a2-b908-4a3e2dca4bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095605} {:answer/id #uuid "b625b0be-89a7-5bb6-a22d-ced7239ee202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095606} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7a7bd7-ebb3-541a-acee-24c94e385e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095607} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6d5ccd-88d5-56e8-b829-d11bb0f681d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095608} {:answer/id #uuid "674bacbd-638e-5a94-93c4-d96b2f53b9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.typ